00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:35.360
So once you have your
VMware environment up

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:38.820
and running in Google
Cloud, a key question is,

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:40.760
how do you enable
data protection

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:43.670
for production workloads?

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:47.810
Backup is the most common
way to achieve that.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:51.500
The good news is that, since the
service offers a native VMware

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:55.250
platform, it is fully compatible
with your existing VMware-based

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:57.920
backup tools, so you
can get into production

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:00.140
quickly with no change.

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:03.650
However, in the cloud,
you can do better.

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.740
In your on-premise
deployments, you often

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:09.830
have secondary storage,
which are lower cost storage

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:11.990
environments to store backups.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:15.650
However, to ensure that you can
survive local site failures,

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:18.950
you often have to do offsite
backups into paid drives,

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:22.410
through paid drives, or
perhaps into the cloud.

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:25.910
But now that your VMware
is literally in the cloud,

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:28.730
there is no need to
make expensive copies

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:30.920
and to ship data out
to multiple locations

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:35.270
to get better availability
and disaster resiliency.

00:20:35.270 --> 00:20:38.090
You can back up your
data into Cloud Storage

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:42.230
and choose how quickly you want
to restore your data, as well

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:47.110
as the redundancy level
of your storage backups.

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:49.570
So for example, you
can choose to have

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:52.120
your data stored in
nearline storage,

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.520
and select
geo-redundancy to ensure

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:58.660
that you can survive a single
site failure at an extremely

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:01.060
low price point.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:05.770
Thus you can get better recovery
times, better availability,

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:09.820
and at much lower cost compared
to your on-premise backup

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:10.987
infrastructure.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:17.080
Now, your VMware infrastructure
has production workloads,

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:20.620
databases, and line of
business applications.

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:23.380
As you look to modernize
this infrastructure,

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:25.660
you want to take
advantage of technologies

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:29.220
like containers and Kubernetes.

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:34.780
Well, Anthos lets you build and
manage container-based, hybrid,

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:37.540
and multicloud applications.

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:41.170
You develop once and
run these applications

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:44.800
across your existing
on-premises infrastructure

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.910
or any major public
cloud provider.

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:52.330
Using Anthos, you can run these
applications on VMware Engine

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:52.890
as well.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.160
Some of the benefits
of doing this

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:02.700
is that you get a dedicated
private environment

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:06.870
with the latest hardware,
100 gigs of networking.

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:10.110
Your workloads can be
right next to the databases

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:13.800
for the lowest latency
and best performance.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:16.500
And since your environment
is single tenant,

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:18.660
you get the benefits
of the privacy

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:21.120
and the security of
dedicated [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:26.850
Now, as you consume
VMware in the cloud,

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:30.620
you also want to ensure that
you avoid the screen swivel

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:33.060
for operations management.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:35.840
For example, if you have
a performance problem,

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:38.630
you want to troubleshoot all
aspects of the application

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:41.450
dependencies in one
place, rather than

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:45.930
go to different systems to solve
different parts of the problem.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:48.030
While the VMware
Engine fully supports

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:52.410
existing VMware monitoring and
logging tools without changes,

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.620
as you start to develop hybrid
applications, for example,

00:22:55.620 --> 00:23:00.660
using Anthos that partially
might live outside of VMware,

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.180
getting all of your
operations data in one place

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:05.450
is going to be important.

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:09.530
To solve this problem, you can
use Google Cloud Operations--

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:12.890
formerly it was known
as Stackdriver--

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:18.230
to collect metrics, logs, traces
across VMware Engine, as well

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:20.630
as other Google Cloud Services.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.660
You can then use the built-in
out-of-the-box dashboards

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:27.170
to view and monitor the
platform and applications.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:29.960
And you can then query
and analyze these signals

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:31.670
intelligently.

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:35.060
And you can see information
from the platform,

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:39.120
from vCenter, from ESXi,
and you can do log analysis.

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:40.850
There's no agent required.

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:46.350
And in fact, VM level data, CPU,
memory, disk, and network usage

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:49.430
is all included, all
available as part

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:51.500
of the signals that come
from the VMware platform

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:55.040
without having to install any
agents inside the workloads

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:57.600
themselves, the VMs themselves.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.720
But if you so want,
you want a deeper view

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:03.570
into the application
metrics, telemetry,

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:05.990
then you can use
open source agents

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:09.650
and collect additional logs
and metrics from inside the VM

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:14.490
and from the application
context directly.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:16.920
So if you want to troubleshoot
any application or gain

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:20.350
insights for
optimization or usage,

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:25.200
you can do it all in one place
in Google Cloud's operations.

00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:31.930
Now, when you peek inside a
typical VMware deployment,

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:34.290
you're going to find that
the top workloads are often

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:39.650
based on databases like
SQL server and Oracle.

00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:43.010
And typically, these are
operational transactional

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:47.690
databases that feed into data
warehousing infrastructures.

00:24:47.690 --> 00:24:50.060
Often, these warehousing
infrastructures

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:53.240
are proprietary and expensive.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.850
They require explicit
infrastructure management

00:24:55.850 --> 00:24:58.260
and maintenance.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:01.170
But Google provides
data services

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:05.460
that simplifies and
supercharges this process.

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:10.920
BigQuery is a serverless, highly
scalable and cost-effective

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:16.650
data warehouse designed to help
you turn big data into informed

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:18.960
business decisions.

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:22.410
It works seamlessly
for real-time analytics

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:27.870
and automatically scales
as your data scales,

00:25:27.870 --> 00:25:31.560
so that your data continues to
make sense to you, without you

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:34.740
having to change your
infrastructure every time you

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:37.080
have a scale change.

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.660
Tools like Tableau
and Data Studio

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:43.440
work seamlessly with BigQuery
to help you better understand

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:45.400
your information.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.930
And you can use services,
such as Cloud Dataflow,

00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:52.440
to incrementally ingest
data, embellish it

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:57.300
with different sources coming
from various different systems,

00:25:57.300 --> 00:26:02.370
clean it dynamically before it's
fed into the BigQuery system.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:06.780
So you can become more
efficient, more intelligent,

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:11.910
and more agile
with data analysis,

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:16.410
and save cost while you edit it.

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:21.180
So as you can see, the service
enables you to not only bring

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:27.420
VMware to Google Cloud, but
also the services and innovation

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:30.720
of Google Cloud to
VMware to give you

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:33.940
